title: 'Request write is unchecked: a daemon that closes before the body is sent kills
  the process with SIGPIPE'
updated: 2026-10-15T04:47:38Z
---

`lib/API/Docker/Role/HTTP.pm:1027` is `print $sock $request;` -- head and the whole body (a build context or `put_archive` tar passed as `raw_body`) in one unchecked `print`. Nothing under `lib/` touches `$SIG{PIPE}` or looks at the return value (`grep -n 'PIPE\|syswrite' lib/API/Docker/Role/HTTP.pm` is empty).

When the daemon answers and closes before it has read the body, the kernel raises SIGPIPE on the next write and the calling program dies with status 141: no exception, no message, no `API::Docker::Error`. Go's `net/http` (dockerd) does exactly that when a handler returns early without draining a large body -- e.g. `containers->put_archive` on a container that does not exist, `images->load`/`build` rejected by an authorization plugin, or the daemon restarting mid-upload.

Reproduced without Docker (a Unix-socket listener that reads 100 bytes, answers 413 and closes):

```
$d->post( '/build', undef, raw_body => 'x' x 20_000_000 );
# process exits with status 141 (SIGPIPE); the eval {} around the call never returns
```

With a small body the same server yields the expected `Docker API error (413)` exception, so the error path itself is fine -- it is only unreachable once the body no longer fits the socket buffer.

Related: because the return value of `print` is ignored, a short write (EPIPE with SIGPIPE ignored by the application, ECONNRESET) goes on to read a response as if the request had been sent; and `read_timeout` (SO_RCVTIMEO) does not bound a write that blocks on a stalled daemon.

Done when: the write is done with `local $SIG{PIPE} = 'IGNORE'` (or `send` with MSG_NOSIGNAL) and its result checked; on EPIPE/ECONNRESET the transport still tries to read the response the daemon may have sent (so the 4xx reaches the caller as `API::Docker::Error::HTTP`) and otherwise croaks a transport error naming the endpoint; a test under `t/` drives the early-close case with a body larger than the socket buffer.

